d obviously but it's one that if  it was released today sure would be a  would have been protected better by g5  yvg those are exactly interesting  so what we were going to do is we're  gonna take advantage of the dirty Cal  vulnerability in the Linux kernel I have  here is a OCI image or an OC I bundle  and in the configuration here we've got  a the UID and GID set to a user and here  we're going to run the a dirty cow  vulnerability and this is going to write  to a file that is controlled by root  from the user from within a container so  when they run this using run C this  takes a little while but the what's  happening here is that we are doing a  couple of Syst calls in parallel it's in  order to trigger a race condition in the  Linux kernel and that's going to allow  us to you write to a file that's  controlled by root and effectively gets  a privilege escalation okay so the the  exploit has run and so what we're gonna  do here is we're gonna check the the  file that we were going to write to so  here we were trying to write to the the  password file in this case our  particular export road to the exploit  Road to the beginning of the password  file so here you can imagine that you  could write in an entirely new line for  our road user and give it a new password  and essentially get privilege escalation  okay so what we're gonna do here is  we're going to run the exact same  vulnerability in the G Weiser sandbox so  here I have the exact same OCI bundle so  we'll look at the two config here we  have the exact same user user ID setup  UID and GID as well as the same exploits  that we're gonna try to run here and so  what we'll do is we'll use the run SC  command to invoke G visor and we will  run this with the P trace and s trace  commands and what this will do is allow  us to see these sis calls that are being  made by the exploit and here we can see  a lot of debug this particular exploit  is running the  is calling these sis calls in a loop in  a tight loop